On Sat, 10 Oct 2026 13:06:31 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> Good start but I think it's doing too much.
>
> I want the interface to be `just am <workspace>=default` and have it
> receive stdin.
>
> I'm okay with there being a helper script or whatever, but I'll be
> calling it from `just am` so that I can run it from the default repo. It
> reads from stdin so that I can use | from notmuch-emacs to pipe the
> current message and apply it.

Thanks. v2 is posted as "[PATCH v2] mail: Add just am to apply patches
in any workspace" [1]. It adds an `am` recipe to the Justfile that
pipes stdin to libexec/mail/am.sh. The recipe is [no-cd], so the
workspace path is relative to where you run it, and the script is
found through justfile_directory(). From notmuch-emacs:

    | just am                # the current directory
    | just am ../mail.jj     # the mail workspace

The default is `.`, per your follow-up.

> Also it doesn't need confirmation output etc, it should work similarly
> to git-am and most other tools in that there's no output if it succeeds.

Done. The Applied and Skipped lines are gone, and the jj commands run
with --quiet. On success it prints nothing. Errors still go to stderr
with a non-zero exit.

